    <p>
      We will answer that in <strong>Where to Stay in Lisbon</strong>.
    </p>

    <h2>Don’t Count Attractions — Choose Experiences</h2>

    <p>
      This may be the most useful way to plan Lisbon and Sintra.
    </p>

    <p>
      Instead of beginning with a checklist of Jerónimos Monastery, Pena Palace,
      São Jorge Castle, a tram ride, Fado, Belém Tower and Quinta da Regaleira,
      begin with the experiences you want the trip to contain.
    </p>

    <p>
      Perhaps you want to experience <strong>old Lisbon</strong>, walking through
      Alfama and looking across the city from its hills.
    </p>

    <p>
      Perhaps you want to understand something about
      <strong>Portugal’s maritime history</strong>, which makes time in Belém
      important.
    </p>

    <p>
      Perhaps you want an evening built around
      <strong>Portuguese food and Fado</strong>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Perhaps you want a day surrounded by
      <strong>Sintra’s palaces, gardens and wooded hills</strong>.
    </p>

    <p>
      And perhaps you want something even simpler:
    </p>

    <p>
      time to sit at a café, walk along the river or turn down a street because it
      looks interesting.
    </p>

    <p>
      Those experiences require time too.
    </p>

    <p>
      They simply do not appear on a sightseeing checklist.
    </p>

    <p>
      A trip becomes more satisfying when you stop asking how many attractions can
      fit into each day and start deciding which experiences deserve a place in the
      trip.
    </p>

    <h2>The Bottom Line</h2>

    <p>
      If you have only <strong>two nights</strong>, concentrate primarily on Lisbon
      unless Sintra is one of your highest priorities.
    </p>

    <p>
      With <strong>three nights</strong>, you can combine Lisbon and Sintra, but you
      will need to make thoughtful choices about what to leave out.
    </p>

    <p>
      With <strong>four nights</strong>, most first-time visitors can achieve a very
      good balance: meaningful time in Lisbon and a full day for Sintra without
      turning every day into a race.
    </p>

    <p>
      With <strong>five nights</strong>, the trip becomes more relaxed, and an
      overnight in Sintra or additional time exploring Lisbon becomes easier to
      consider.
    </p>

    <p>
      With <strong>six nights or more</strong>, Lisbon can become a base for
      experiencing more of the surrounding region.
    </p>

    <p>
      There will always be another palace, museum, viewpoint, neighborhood or
      restaurant you could add.
    </p>

    <p>
      You do not need to see all of them.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        <strong>
          Portugal rewards travelers who leave themselves enough time to experience
          a place rather than simply prove they were there.
        </strong>
      </p>
    </blockquote>

